WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
The natural world is full of extraordinary animals with amazing life histories.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:13.000
Yet certain stories are more intriguing than most.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:22.000
The mysteries of a butterfly's life cycle, or the strange biology of the emperor penguin.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:29.000
Some of these creatures were surrounded by myth and misunderstandings for a very long time.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:34.000
And some have only recently revealed their secrets.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.000
These are the animals that stand out from the crowd.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:43.000
The curiosities I find particularly fascinating.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:58.000
In this program we meet two animals whose extraordinary body shapes are determined by their diet.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:04.000
The blue whale grows enormous by feeding on tiny shrimp-like creatures,

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:10.000
while flamingos spend their lives eating with their heads upside down.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:14.000
And yet both ways are curiously similar.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:25.000
The blue whale weighs almost 200 tons.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:29.000
It's the largest animal on earth and it's rarely seen.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:34.000
I didn't glimpse one until I had been filming animals for almost 50 years,

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:39.000
and when I did it was one of the greatest thrills of my life.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:50.000
I can see its tail just under my boat here.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:52.000
And it's coming up, it's coming up.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:54.000
There!

00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:01.000
The blue whale is 100 feet long, 30 meters.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:09.000
Nothing like that can grow on them because no bone is strong enough to support such a bulk.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:24.000
Only in the sea can you get such huge size as that magnificent creature.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:28.000
The blue whale was a mystery to science for a long time.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:35.000
Living out in the deep oceans, people rarely caught sight of more than the spout of this giant.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:40.000
The first published description comes from a physician, Robert Sibold,

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:44.000
who found a whale stranded off the coast of Scotland.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.000
The whale was found in the deep ocean,

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:51.000
and the blue whale was found in the deep ocean.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:57.000
He found a whale stranded off the coast of Scotland in 1692.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:05.000
It was first named after Sibold, but later given the scientific name Ballinoptera musculus.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:09.000
The Latin musculus means both muscle and little mouse,

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:14.000
an ironic double meaning for the largest animal on earth.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:20.000
When the first blue whale specimens were washed up on our shores,

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:24.000
they must have caused quite a stir and excitement.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.000
Here was a colossal animal weighing over 150 tons.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:31.000
Nothing as big had ever been seen before.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:37.000
A giant of this scale must be a predator at the top of the food chain.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:42.000
But what kind of creature was it, and what was it feeding on to make it so big?

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:50.000
The first blue whale specimens were found at a time when scientists were just starting to classify animals

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:55.000
not only by their external appearance, but by their internal structures.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:04:00.000
And few animals proved as problematic as the whales.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.000
From the outside, they looked and behaved like fish,

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:12.000
but their internal organs were like those of a large mammal.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:17.000
The bones of the whale's front fins are very similar to those in our own arms.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:21.000
The five digits on the hand are clearly visible,

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:25.000
but they've been modified into paddles for swimming.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:32.000
What kind of creatures were these truly extraordinary animals?

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:43.000
The controversy as to whether whales were fish or mammals came to a head in a New York courtroom in 1818.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:49.000
A jury was asked to pass judgment on the question for the purpose of the New York state law.

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:58.000
The issue had come up because a shrewd merchant who owned three barrels of whale oil had refused to pay tax levied on fish oil.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:04.000
He pointed out that, according to the latest scientific opinion, whales weren't in fact fish.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:08.000
The inspector collecting the tax had scorned the idea.

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:13.000
What, whales not fish, he said, and slapped handcuffs on the merchant.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:19.000
The lead witness was a respected scientist called Samuel Mitchell.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:26.000
Mitchell entered the courtroom expecting to explain to everybody why whales were mammals, not fish,

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:34.000
but instead found himself being attacked by the most gifted lawyer in the country, William Sampson.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:43.000
Sampson argued that scientists didn't have the right to rename God's creatures and force them into absurd groupings.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:50.000
The idea that humans and whales should be in the same category seemed to him grotesque.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:55.000
Mitchell and Sampson never stood a chance.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:06:02.000
After deliberating for 15 minutes, the jury announced the verdict in favor of Sampson and the fish oil inspector.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:08.000
According to New York State law, whales were deemed to be fish, not mammals.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:14.000
Although the general public still considered whales to be fish,

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:22.000
scientists were by now largely agreed that they were indeed mammals that had taken to living in the sea.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:30.000
But what was the blue whale feeding on to allow it to grow to such an extraordinary size?

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:38.000
The answer can be found by looking inside the mouth, which contains some very bizarre looking structures.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:47.000
This is the skeleton of a right whale, and its mouth parts are very similar to those of the blue whale.

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:53.000
Instead of teeth, it has these strange plate-like structures hanging from the upper jaw.

00:06:53.000 --> 00:07:01.000
The plates are aligned alongside each other, and the inner edges fray because the large tongue continually rubs up against them.

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:09.000
And then the frayed edges entangle to form a thick mat that acts like a gigantic sieve.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:14.000
And when early naturalists opened up the gigantic guts of these whales,

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:21.000
they found not fish or other large prey, but tiny shrimp-like creatures called krill.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:28.000
To everyone's astonishment, it turned out that these whales feed on some of the smallest prey in the sea,

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:34.000
and these strange plates serve to filter the krill out of the water.

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:44.000
The rows of plates are called baleen, and we now know that they form a highly specialised filter-feeding system.

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:51.000
The giant animal dives deep beneath the surface in search of swarms of krill.

00:07:53.000 --> 00:08:03.000
The preated skin on the throat and belly expand, and the mouth balloons outward to four times the size, taking in an enormous mouthful of water.

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:13.000
The tongue then forces the water out through the baleen, and thousands of tiny krill are left behind.

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:21.000
Today, we know a lot more about this unusual feeding structure.

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:30.000
This is baleen. It's often referred to as whale bone, but it's not bone at all.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:38.000
It's keratin, the same substance as our hair and fingernails, and it's both strong and slightly elastic.

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:45.000
The plates emerge from the whale's jaws instead of teeth and continue to grow throughout the whale's lifetime.

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:49.000
These bands in it are much like the rings of a tree.

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:55.000
Several may be laid down the course of a year, so the baleen can give us an indication of the age of a whale.

00:08:55.000 --> 00:09:01.000
We also know from other evidence that blue whales can live to be over 100 years old.

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:09.000
Recently discovered fossil whales have both teeth and simple filters,

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:15.000
which suggests that early filter-feeding whales may have sucked small animals from the seafloor.

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:23.000
There is a whale alive today that feeds in just that way, the grey whale.

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:32.000
It stirs up the sediment and scoops it into its mouth and then filters out small food particles with its baleen.

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:49.000
Krill is abundant in the oceans, and blue whales can eat enormous quantities of it with each mouthful,

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:54.000
soon swallowing 90 times more than they immediately need.

00:09:54.000 --> 00:10:01.000
The surplus is then stored in the form of blubber, and this helps them cope with periods when food is scarce.

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:09.000
The blue whale was a mystery to us for a long time,

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:16.000
but we now know that its enormous body is fuelled with vast quantities of the tiniest of prey.

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:24.000
Over the course of its lifetime, a blue whale will consume around 50,000 tonnes of krill.

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:31.000
And unlike teeth, which fall out with old age, the baleen never stops growing and is constantly replaced.

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:37.000
Maybe this unusual body design not only helps the blue whale grow to this enormous size,

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:41.000
but also to such a formidable old age.

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:51.000
In part two, we meet another filter feeder and discover how its unique diet has affected both its body design and lifestyle.

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:08.000
The blue whale has become a giant by filtering tiny creatures out of the ocean.

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:17.000
Our second curiosity, the flamingo, also has an unusual body that has been shaped by its diet.

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:25.000
For a long time, the flamingos were birds of myth and mystery.

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:35.000
Travellers in Africa saw them shrouded by the hazy mists rising from volcanic soda lakes and believed that they were firebirds.

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:43.000
In Egyptian mythology, the firebird, or phoenix, was a sacred creature with beautiful red plumage

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:49.000
that was consumed by magical fire and then rose again from its own ashes.

00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:56.000
The flamingo's scientific name, Phenicopterus, reflects some of its legendary past.

00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:59.000
It means phoenix wing.

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:05.000
These beautiful and elegant creatures are some of the most curious-looking of birds.

00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:12.000
No other bird has a beak shaped quite like this, or indeed such glorious pink colours.

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:17.000
And yet we're so familiar with them that we rarely think about their strange appearance.

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:23.000
Why is it that the flamingo is so different from all other birds?

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:31.000
In that classic children's book Alice in Wonderland, Lewis Carroll has fun with the flamingo's oddity.

00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:37.000
Alice plays croquet with the red queen, using them as mallets,

00:12:37.000 --> 00:12:44.000
holding their heads and necks upside down in much the same posture as the birds take when feeding.

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:55.000
When you look at the skeleton of a flamingo, the thing that strikes you most is the extraordinary length of the legs and the neck.

00:12:55.000 --> 00:13:03.000
The neck has 17 bones in it, which is no more than in other birds, but each is greatly elongated,

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:08.000
giving the flamingo its extra long neck and flexibility.

00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:21.000
But the flamingo's most curious feature is surely its beak, and the reason it looks so strange is that it is the only beak adapted for use upside down.

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:30.000
In most birds, the upper part of the bill is larger than the lower one, but in the flamingo's it's the other way round.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:38.000
The lower bill is much bigger and has a deep central groove in it that holds the flamingo's tongue.

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:51.000
The upper jaw is thin and moveable, so when the bird's head is upside down, the flamingo's jaws work as it were normally.

00:13:51.000 --> 00:14:05.000
When feeding, the flamingo gently sweeps its bill back and forth, sucking water in at the front and squirting it out from the sides.

00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:19.000
The water that goes in is murky, while that which flows out is clear, and that gives us a clue to what it's feeding on.

00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:29.000
The beak has tiny bristles all along its edges, much like the whale's baleen.

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:36.000
And the tongue has two rows of horny spikes along its length.

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:47.000
When feeding, the bristles and spikes form a sieve, trapping any particles inside.

00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:54.000
And the large tongue acts as a pump, pushing water in and out.

00:14:54.000 --> 00:15:03.000
It's a unique design for a beak. No other bird has one like it.

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:14.000
Although at first sight they may look the same, flamingo beaks in fact come in two different shapes.

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:18.000
And this is because they eat slightly different food.

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:30.000
This is the beak of a greater flamingo, which feeds on crustaceans, which are usually found near the bottom of a lake.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:36.000
It's long and shallow, so the birds can feed in water only a few millimetres deep.

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:46.000
This beak on the other hand is from a lesser flamingo. Its bill is shorter, but more bulbous and deep-keeled.

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:53.000
The lesser flamingo feeds on microscopic algae, which usually float just below the surface of the water.

00:15:53.000 --> 00:16:02.000
And the deep keel acts as a buoy, bobbing along just at the right depth as the bird moves through the water.

00:16:02.000 --> 00:16:12.000
These different bills allow two species of flamingo to live side by side.

00:16:12.000 --> 00:16:24.000
In Africa's Rift Valley, greater and lesser flamingos are found on the Soda Lakes, having specialised on food that others can't reach.

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:34.000
The waters are so hot and toxic that they would strip the flesh off any other animal, but flamingos thrive here.

00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:46.000
Their long spindly legs have tough scales, and their webbed feet prevent them from sinking into the soft mud.

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:53.000
The birds can even drink the water, which is two or three times saltier than the ocean.

00:16:53.000 --> 00:16:59.000
But it's not just the mud and water which are poisonous.

00:16:59.000 --> 00:17:08.000
The blue-green algae, which many often feed on, actually contain nasty toxic chemicals.

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:13.000
If that were to accumulate in the internal organs of the bird, they could be lethal.

00:17:13.000 --> 00:17:21.000
But the flamingo deals with that by directing these chemicals into the feathers and the skin, where they do no damage.

00:17:21.000 --> 00:17:31.000
The feathers of flamingos contain very high concentrations of toxins, but they also contain another chemical, keratin.

00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:40.000
Keratin is the reddish pigment that gives flamingos their distinctive pink colour, and it also comes from their diet.

00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:49.000
But keratin is not harmful. On the contrary, it's a source of vitamin A and boosts the immune system, protecting against illness.

00:17:49.000 --> 00:17:55.000
So a pink bird is also a healthy bird.

00:17:55.000 --> 00:18:03.000
This glorious pink colour was probably an incidental by-product of their diet.

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:10.000
Nonetheless, over time, it has evolved to play an important role in the flamingo's social life.

00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:16.000
The flashes of colour are an integral part of their courtship display,

00:18:16.000 --> 00:18:24.000
and recent research has shown that the pinkest flamingos are the most popular when it comes to finding a mate.

00:18:29.000 --> 00:18:36.000
When flamingos breed, much of the keratin in their diet gets channeled into the developing young.

00:18:36.000 --> 00:18:43.000
Even the eggs receive pigments, so much, in fact, that the yolk can be virtually blood-red in colour.

00:18:43.000 --> 00:18:49.000
These eggs are from captive flamingos and are infertile.

00:18:49.000 --> 00:18:52.000
Let's have a look.

00:18:52.000 --> 00:18:56.000
There.

00:18:56.000 --> 00:19:02.000
Well, it's nothing like the colour of any other bird yolk that I've ever seen.

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:10.000
Flamingos are so efficient at collecting their specialised food that the yolk is actually packed full of protein and fat.

00:19:10.000 --> 00:19:16.000
This allows the chick to grow particularly quickly and gives it a good start in life.

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:26.000
Despite the colour of the yolk, the chicks hatch with fluffy grey feathers.

00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:31.000
They're fed on special milk from their parents' crop.

00:19:31.000 --> 00:19:38.000
This is not regurgitated food, but a secretion produced by the lining of the digestive tract.

00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:42.000
And it's deep red in colour.

00:19:50.000 --> 00:19:55.000
The flamingo chick relies on this for the first few weeks of its life,

00:19:55.000 --> 00:20:00.000
and it will eventually enable it to grow its glorious pink plumes.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:06.000
We now know that much of the flamingo's bizarre appearance has been shaped by its diet.

00:20:06.000 --> 00:20:09.000
The one question continues to battle scientists.

00:20:09.000 --> 00:20:13.000
To which group of birds do the flamingos actually belong?

00:20:13.000 --> 00:20:20.000
Some thought that they must be related to ducks and geese because of their webbed feet and short duck-like beaks.

00:20:20.000 --> 00:20:26.000
But others were convinced that with their long legs they're more like waders, such as storks.

00:20:26.000 --> 00:20:32.000
Recent DNA studies contradict both these suggestions.

00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:41.000
They reveal that the flamingo's closest relative may in fact be a small diving bird that looks nothing like a flamingo.

00:20:41.000 --> 00:20:45.000
The grebe.

00:20:47.000 --> 00:20:54.000
Further studies found other similarities in the structure of the eye and the shape of the beak.

00:20:54.000 --> 00:21:01.000
Similarities in the structure of the eye and the number of feathers on the wing.

00:21:02.000 --> 00:21:08.000
So it seems that flamingos and grebes are indeed each other's closest relatives.

00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:18.000
But over time, diet and lifestyle has shaped the flamingo into a very different looking bird, far removed from its grebe-like ancestor.

00:21:18.000 --> 00:21:27.000
It's fair to say there's nothing else quite like a flamingo.

00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:32.000
The flamingo and the blue whale are two very different creatures.

00:21:32.000 --> 00:21:36.000
One living on land and one in the deep oceans.

00:21:36.000 --> 00:21:45.000
And yet their bodies have been shaped in a similar way by their diet, making each of them a curiosity within its own mind.

00:21:45.000 --> 00:21:50.000
Making each of them a curiosity within its own group.

00:22:15.000 --> 00:22:18.000
Thank you for watching!
